#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SESSION_NOTES-S231-through-S228.md.verify.sh — the losslessness proof for this shard.
#
# Generated by Session 235 (the FIFTH trim). Re-runnable, read-only, exits non-zero on failure.
#
#   bash <this>              prove the fifth trim was lossless AND landed where it was declared
#   bash <this> --self-test  prove this proof can FAIL (59 mutants; exit 2 if any survives)
#
# Descended from Session 231's SESSION_NOTES-S227-through-S225.md.verify.sh, itself descended from
# Session 228's, Session 224's and Session 222's — read those for the rationale behind L0-L3, L4,
# L5-L7 and L8-L9. Shards are WRITE-ONCE: this proof pins ITS OWN cut key and does not re-prove the
# earlier shards' cuts. Run all five.
#
# ONE INHERITED ASSERTION IS NARROWED HERE, and it is the most important paragraph in this header.
#
#   L2/b3 (NO-LEAK) previously scanned `shard_front + "".join(shard_recs)` for front-matter lines.
#   THAT PREDICATE FIRES ON THIS CUT, AND IT IS WRONG TO. Measured before anything was written:
#   front-matter line 54 — the third trim's markdown link
#   `[`docs/architecture-history/SESSION_NOTES-S224-through-S221.md`](docs/…-S224-through-S221.md)`
#   — is a proper substring of line 1542, which sits inside Session 228's record and therefore moves
#   into this shard. Same text, two legitimate homes: a front-matter block citing a shard, and a
#   session record citing the same shard. No trim before this one had a collision at all, so no
#   ancestor could have discovered it.
#
#   THE OBSERVED FAILURE IS NOT THE ARGUMENT — it is only what sent someone looking. Tightening an
#   instrument because it just went red is how a gate gets calibrated to noise, so the collision
#   below is reported as motivation and carries no weight. The argument is the next paragraph, and
#   it would hold on a cut where nothing collided at all.
#
#   AN EXACT-LINE PREDICATE WAS THE OTHER AVAILABLE FIX, and it was measured before being rejected:
#   `L2/b2` three lines above already uses exact membership, commented "never substring (BL-28)",
#   and under exact-line matching this cut's collision disappears (1 hit -> 0) and all 59 mutants
#   still pass. It was rejected because it fixes the WRONG THING. It makes the false positive
#   rarer, not impossible — a record may quote a pointer-block line verbatim on its own line, and
#   0 such lines today is a fact about today — while leaving in place a scan that, by the argument
#   below, can never produce a TRUE positive under ANY predicate. Keeping a check that can only
#   ever be wrong, less often, is not an improvement.
#
#   THE FIX IS NOT AN ALLOWLIST EITHER, and the narrowing is not a weakening. Scanning the RECORDS
#   half for a leak is
#   structurally incapable of a true positive: L1 asserts
#   `"".join(after_recs) + "".join(shard_recs) == "".join(before_recs)` by exact byte equality, so
#   ANY text inserted into a record — front-matter line or otherwise — moves those bytes and trips
#   L1 first. A front-matter line found inside `shard_recs` was therefore already inside
#   `before_recs`; it is history, not a leak. The only place a leak can hide from L1 is the BANNER,
#   which is new text L1 never sees and which L6 pins only against a declaration THE SAME AUTHOR
#   wrote — so if the author pastes a front-matter line into BANNER, L6 is satisfied and b3 is the
#   only assertion left able to object. That is not hypothetical: the fourth trim's b3 fired on its
#   first run, and it fired ON ITS BANNER (its own note at BANNER's "One clause per line" aside).
#   So `body` is now `shard_front` alone — the region where the assertion can actually work.
#
#   NOTHING IS HIDDEN BY THE NARROWING. The run prints, as a NOTE and never as a failure, every
#   front-matter line the OLD predicate would have flagged, so a reader sees exactly what changed
#   and can judge it. And b3 keeps a mutant that no other assertion catches (M51), because an arm
#   that survives a narrowing without one is the defect this lineage has now found three times.
#
# TWO ASSERTIONS ARE NEW HERE. Neither is speculative: each closes a hole an earlier proof NAMED IN
# ITS OWN PROSE and then declined to check.
#
#   L10 (PROVENANCE) — every ancestor shard's PROOF SCRIPT, held against a hand-declared freeze
#      commit. L9's own docstring says it holds the ancestors' artifacts because that is "a
#      precondition for running them at all". It is one of two. The other is the proof itself, and
#      nothing guards it: truncate, weaken or delete `SESSION_NOTES-through-S216.md.verify.sh` and
#      every assertion in this suite stays green while the routing table and four banners keep
#      telling readers to run it. A weakened proof is worse than a deleted one — it exits 0.
#
#      IT IS DECLARED, NOT DERIVED, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY. The obvious design — hold each proof
#      against the blob at the commit that ADDED it, exactly as L9 holds the shards — is RED on
#      arrival. Measured: the S227 proof has two commits, `f3fea4e` (its trim) and `e314449`
#      (Session 231's own close-out, which reworded two `print` lines), so its add-blob and its disk
#      bytes differ today. Proof scripts are frozen when their SESSION ends, not when the file is
#      created; there is no commit git can derive that means that. So the freeze point is
#      hand-declared per proof, like the cut key — an assertion, not a restatement — and a later
#      edit to any ancestor proof turns this red.
#
#   L11 (POLICY) — that this cut obeyed the retention rule it exists to satisfy. Every assertion
#      above is about whether the trim was performed FAITHFULLY. None is about whether it was
#      performed AT ALL: a trim that archived one record would be perfectly lossless, land its
#      routing table, pass L0-L10 green, and leave the file over the trigger that fired it. The
#      fourth trim's closing text says the suite "says NOTHING about whether the boundary was WISELY
#      chosen". Wisdom is not mechanizable; the POLICY half of it is three numbers in `CLAUDE.md`,
#      and those are.
#
#      IT CHECKS THE NUMBERS AGAINST THE SENTENCE THAT DECLARES THEM, which is the same move L5
#      makes for the routing table. Three magic integers in a script are worth little: a future
#      trim could relax them here and be green. So the declared trigger/target/floor must also
#      appear, formatted, in `CLAUDE.md`'s retention bullet. Two independent halves, four arms, four
#      mutants — see M56-M59.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE — every line below was produced by running the loops, not by predicting them.
# Neuter one assertion at a time and re-run --self-test; what survives is what it ALONE catches:
#
#     for A in L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 L5 L6 L7 L8 L9 L10 L11; do
#       sed "s|^    fails += $A(.*|    pass|" <this> > /tmp/n.sh
#       echo "$A: $(bash /tmp/n.sh --self-test | grep SURVIVED)"; done
#
#     L0 (none)   L1 (none)*  L2 M10,M12-M15,M40,M51   L3 (none)   L4 (none)
#
#     * L1's blank entry is NOT a licence to remove it. L3 and L4 happen to catch every mutant
#       L1 catches, so nothing depends on L1 ALONE — but L1 is the assertion the narrowing of
#       L2/b3 rests on, and b3's records half is dead only while L1 runs and TRANSFORM is None.
#       Read '(none)' here as 'overlapped', never as 'removable'.
#     L5 M19-M26,M38,M39,M47,M52   L6 M27,M28   L7 M29,M30   L8 M31-M34,M41-M46,M49,M50
#     L9 M35-M37,M48   L10 M53-M55   L11 M56-M59
#
# THAT LOOP IS NOT ENOUGH, and this lineage has now proved it three times over: the SECOND trim
# (Session 224) shipped `L2/b0` reachable by no mutant, the THIRD (Session 228) shipped `L5/2`'s
# partition arm reachable by no mutant — both measured, not cited, by re-running those two
# ancestors' own self-tests — and
# mutant, and an adversarial sweep of the fourth's own file then found FOUR MORE unmutated arms it
# had shipped. An assertion is a handful of separate `if`s; neutering the whole function cannot
# tell you that one of those `if`s has never fired. So neuter one `if` at a time as well. All 34
# arms were swept that way here. The mutant each arm alone catches:
#
#     L2/b3 M51        L5/1 M19-M23,M26   L5/2 loop M39      L5/4 stray M25
#     L7/differs M29   L8/required M50    L8/forbidden M44   L8/set M45,M46
#     L9/differs M35,M37   L10/differs M55
#     L11/fire M56     L11/target M57     L11/floor M58      L11/declared M59
#
# SEVEN ARMS CRASH THE RUN WHEN NEUTERED rather than surviving, because the arm below them then
# dereferences a None or a sentinel. Read a traceback as `caught`, not as `passed`:
#     L5/2 shape   L7/missing   L8/unreadable   L9/no-add   L9/missing
#     L10/no-freeze   L10/missing
#
# THIRTEEN ARMS HAVE NO UNIQUELY-CATCHING MUTANT, and saying which — and why — is more useful than
# inventing contrived ones. This list is measured; do not trust it after editing anything:
#
#   * L0/empty — no artifact mutation can reach it. Emptying a records zone moves bytes, so L1, L3
#     and L4 fire first. It guards a GRAMMAR break (someone editing RECORD_START), which is not
#     expressible as a mutation of the artifacts. It is a guard rail, and that is the honest name.
#   * L2/b0 — M14, M15 and M40 fire it, but each also breaks b1 and b2, so none depends on it.
#     THE FOURTH TRIM'S HEADER CLAIMS OTHERWISE — its coverage line reads `L2/b0 M14,M15`, listing
#     it among the arms with a uniquely-catching mutant. That is false, and measurably: neuter
#     line 492 of `SESSION_NOTES-S227-through-S225.md.verify.sh` and all 50 of its mutants are
#     still caught. Kept here anyway, because b0 NAMES the cause where b1 only shows bytes.
#   * L2/pointer, L2/b1, L2/b2 — mutually covering. M8 trips pointer and L5/1; M10-M13 trip b1+b2.
#   * L3/ORDER — M5 also trips L1 and L4/archived.
#   * L4/retained, L4/archived — subsumed by L5/4 since the third trim added it, as that trim said.
#     Kept: L4 states the cut key directly instead of inferring it, and names both sides on failure.
#   * L5/2 final — STRUCTURALLY subsumed, and this is new. The final junction is the one between the
#     shard clause and the live clause, and L5/3 pins BOTH of those exactly, so every single-clause
#     mutation there trips L5/3 as well. The fourth trim shipped this arm with no mutant and no
#     explanation; the explanation is that no mutant is possible while L5/3 stands.
#   * L5/3 shard, L5/3 live — M24 also trips L5/4.
#   * L5/4 unreadable, L5/4 vacuous — mutually covering for a `None` result: neuter either and the
#     other catches M52; neuter both and the run crashes on `for n in found`. M52 IS THE FIRST
#     MUTANT IN THIS LINEAGE TO REACH THAT PATH AT ALL — the fourth trim's fifty reached neither
#     arm, which the sweep of its file measured and its own header did not know.
#
# Those are MEASURED, not predicted, and the first draft of this file was wrong twice in ways only
# running it exposed. M52 was written to be uniquely-L5/4 and named NINE assertions, because a
# whole-text filename replace also rewrote the archived records (moving bytes, tripping L1 and L3)
# and because L8's derived half closed over the module-level ROUTING instead of the declaration a
# mutant had moved. Both were fixed — the mutant narrowed to the routing clause, L8 given the
# declared table L5 reads. Run the loops; do not reason about them.
#
# The grammar is FROZEN into this file on purpose: a later change to the trim design must not
# silently change what THIS shard's proof means.
set -euo pipefail
exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
